<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Melbourne</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Melbourne is the capital of Victoria and the second-largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, on Port Phillip Bay at the mouth of the Yarra River. Founded in 1835 by settlers from Van Diemen's Land, it boomed after the gold rushes of the 1850s to become one of the largest cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>. It served as the seat of the federal government from 1901 until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canberra">Canberra</a> replaced it in 1927.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/melbourne.jpg" alt="Melbourne" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Melbpal · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Melbourne_skyline_sor.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Melbourne occupies much of the northern and eastern coastlines of Port Phillip Bay, in the southeastern part of mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>. The Boonwurrung and Woiwurrung languages call the area Narrm or Naarm; in Boonwurrung, Naarm means the Bay. The city is named after William Lamb, 2nd Viscount Melbourne, who was Prime Minister of the United Kingdom when it was founded in 1835. At its centre is the Hoddle Grid, a grid of streets measuring about 1.61 by 0.80 kilometres, whose southern edge fronts onto the Yarra River.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aboriginal-australians">Aboriginal Australians</a> have lived in the Melbourne area for at least 40,000 years. When British colonists arrived in the 19th century, up to 20,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulin">Kulin</a> people from three language groups, the Wurundjeri, Bunurong and Wathaurong, lived there. The area was an important meeting place for the clans of the Kulin nation alliance and a vital source of food and water. A short-lived British penal settlement was founded at Port Phillip in 1803, then part of the Colony of New South Wales. Melbourne itself was founded in 1835 with the arrival of free settlers from Van Diemen's Land, now Tasmania, and was incorporated as a Crown settlement in 1837.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> declared it a city in 1847, and in 1851 it became the capital of the newly separated Colony of Victoria. The Victorian gold rush of the 1850s began a long boom which, by the late 1880s, had made it one of the largest and wealthiest cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and the world. After the federation of Australia in 1901 it was the interim seat of government until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canberra">Canberra</a> became the permanent capital in 1927. In 1956 it hosted the Summer Olympics, the first Olympic Games held outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Melbourne is the capital of the state of Victoria and the second most populous city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, with more than five million people. It is known for the Victorian architecture left by the gold rush and the land boom of the 1880s; the Royal Exhibition Building, completed in 1880, is World Heritage listed. Little survives from before the gold rush, St James Old Cathedral (1839) and St Francis' Church (1845) being among the few examples in the city centre.</p><p>The city gave rise to Australian rules football, Australian impressionism and Australian cinema. The Melbourne Cup horse race, first held in 1861, was declared a public holiday for all Melburnians in 1873. The University of Melbourne is the second-oldest university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Melbourne” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Parliament sat in <b>Melbourne</b> until 1927, when it moved to Canberra, a new capital built on federal territory between Sydney and Melbourne as a compromise between the two rival cities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Commonwealth of Australia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The first exhibition outside China was held at the National Gallery of Victoria in <b>Melbourne</b> in 1982.</blockquote><a href="/work/terracotta-army">Terracotta Army · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Botany Bay&quot; can be traced back to Little Jack Sheppard, a musical burlesque staged at the Gaiety Theatre in London in 1885 and in <b>Melbourne</b> in 1886.</blockquote><a href="/songs/botany-bay">Botany Bay</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1876 The Saturday Advertiser held a competition to set it to music, judged by three prominent <b>Melbourne</b> musicians, with a prize of ten guineas.</blockquote><a href="/songs/god-defend-new-zealand">God Defend New Zealand</a></li><li><blockquote>That version was used for 47 years, until Addie MS recorded a new one in <b>Melbourne</b> in 1998 with his Twilite Orchestra, the Victorian Philharmonic Orchestra and the Melbourne Symphony Orchestra.</blockquote><a href="/songs/indonesia-raya">Indonesia Raya</a></li><li><blockquote>Settlers sent by John Pascoe Fawkner went ashore on the Yarra River, building the first houses of what became <b>Melbourne</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-30">30 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
